@page "/docs/registry/getting-started"
<PageTitle>Registry: Getting Started - Blaizio</PageTitle>

<div class="typeset typeset-docs space-y-10">
    <header class="docs-page-header space-y-2.5">
        <div class="flex flex-wrap items-center gap-2">
            <h1 class="text-2xl font-bold tracking-tight">Getting Started</h1>
            <BzBadge Variant="BadgeVariant.Secondary">Registry</BzBadge>
        </div>
        <p class="max-w-3xl">From a folder of components to a URL other projects can install from. Four commands, and a static host at the end of them.</p>
    </header>

    <section>
        <h2 data-toc class="scroll-mt-20">Lay out the source</h2>
        <p>A registry is an ordinary repository. The manifest sits at the root; item files live wherever you like, as long as the paths in the manifest are relative to it. The build output is the only part that gets hosted.</p>
        <CodeBlock Snippet="Registry/Layout" Language="text" />
        <p>One list is fine to start with. When it stops being fine, split it: <code>include</code> folds a manifest per folder into the root one, covered on the <a href="docs/registry/registry-json">manifest reference</a>.</p>
    </section>

    <section>
        <h2 data-toc class="scroll-mt-20">Write the components</h2>
        <p>Author under the <code>Blaizio.Ui</code> root namespace, the same convention the official registry uses. On install the CLI rewrites that prefix to the consumer's namespace (<code>MyApp.Components.Ui.&lt;YourRegistry&gt;</code>), so cross-references between your own components keep compiling wherever they land. <a href="docs/registry/author-a-component">Author a component</a> walks one component from the first line to the install, with the conventions that make it fit.</p>
        <p>Reference the headless layer (<code>Blaizio.Base</code>, <code>Blaizio.Icons</code>) as NuGet dependencies on the item. Never copy Base sources into a registry: the consumer already has that package, and a copied primitive stops receiving fixes.</p>
    </section>

    <section>
        <h2 data-toc class="scroll-mt-20">Generate, validate, build</h2>
        <p>For a tree of components, <CmdChip Command="blaizio generate" /> writes the manifest for you: one item per component folder, with dependencies between your own components inferred from the <code>Bz*</code> types each file references, and the official components you build on named for you to add as <code>@("@default/&lt;item&gt;")</code>. Hand-writing it is fine too, and easier for themes.</p>
        <CodeBlock Snippet="Registry/BuildValidate" />
        <p><CmdChip Command="blaizio registry validate" /> is the gate worth putting in CI: it checks the manifest parses, that names are unique, that theme and font items carry their payloads, and that every declared file exists on disk. <CmdChip Command="blaizio build" /> then inlines each file's contents into a per-item JSON and writes the <code>index.json</code> catalogue beside them.</p>
    </section>

    <section>
        <h2 data-toc class="scroll-mt-20">Host the output</h2>
        <p>Anything that serves files over HTTPS will do. The CLI only ever issues GETs for <code>&lt;base&gt;/index.json</code> and <code>&lt;base&gt;/&lt;name&gt;.json</code>. A GitHub Pages workflow that validates then builds on every push is the whole pipeline:</p>
        <CodeBlock Snippet="Registry/Workflow" Language="yaml" />
        <p>Serve the output over HTTPS with permissive CORS if you expect browser tooling to read it. A local folder path works as a registry too, which is the fastest way to test one before it is published anywhere.</p>
    </section>

    <section>
        <h2 data-toc class="scroll-mt-20">Try it as a consumer</h2>
        <p>Point a scratch project at the built output, either the deployed URL or the local directory, and install something. What consumers do from here is covered on <a href="docs/registry/namespaces">Namespaces</a>.</p>
        <CodeBlock Snippet="Registry/ConsumerAdd" />
    </section>
</div>
